 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "At 5 a.m., Sweden's drivers all switched sides of the road.",
   "s": 0.12,
   "e": 3.116
  },
  {
   "text": "In 1967, every car in the country stopped, crept right, and waited.",
   "s": 3.416,
   "e": 7.466
  },
  {
   "text": "Sweden drove on the left. But most of its cars were built for the right.",
   "s": 7.766,
   "e": 10.924
  },
  {
   "text": "In 1955, 83 percent of Swedes voted against switching.",
   "s": 11.224,
   "e": 14.775
  },
  {
   "text": "So the government did it anyway.",
   "s": 15.075,
   "e": 16.363
  },
  {
   "text": "Then it got worse: 360,000 road signs had to flip in about a day.",
   "s": 16.663,
   "e": 20.624
  },
  {
   "text": "Buses got new doors. A pop song begged, \"Keep to the right, Svensson!\"",
   "s": 20.924,
   "e": 24.185
  },
  {
   "text": "Everyone expected crashes everywhere. Instead, crashes went down for a while.",
   "s": 24.365,
   "e": 28.217
  },
  {
   "text": "Turns out, scared drivers are careful drivers.",
   "s": 28.517,
   "e": 30.581
  }
 ],
